-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 1: BACnet Device Discovery and Who-Is Troubleshooting
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'troubleshooting'),
  'BACnet Device Discovery and Who-Is Troubleshooting',
  'bacnet-device-discovery-who-is-troubleshooting',
  'Systematic troubleshooting for BACnet device discovery failures using Who-Is/I-Am, covering device instance conflicts, network number issues, BBMD gaps, and router misconfigurations.',
  E'# BACnet Device Discovery and Who-Is Troubleshooting\n\nWhen a BACnet device does not appear in a supervisor or workstation discovery scan, the instinct is to blame the device. In practice, the problem is usually a network configuration issue that prevents the Who-Is broadcast from reaching the device or the I-Am response from returning.\n\n## How BACnet Discovery Works\n\nBACnet device discovery uses two unconfirmed services defined in ASHRAE Standard 135:\n\n- **Who-Is**: A broadcast request asking devices to identify themselves. Can target all devices, a specific device instance, or a range of device instances.\n- **I-Am**: A broadcast response containing the device''s object identifier, maximum APDU length, segmentation support, and vendor identifier. Devices also broadcast I-Am on power-up.\n\nBecause both services are unconfirmed (no application-layer acknowledgment), there is no retry mechanism built into the protocol. If the broadcast does not reach the target or the response does not return, discovery silently fails.\n\n## Prerequisite Checks\n\nBefore troubleshooting discovery, verify the fundamentals:\n\n| Check | How |\n|---|---|\n| Device is powered and running | Status LEDs, ping if IP |\n| Device is on the correct network | Physical wiring or IP subnet |\n| BACnet service is enabled | Device configuration tool |\n| UDP port 47808 is open | Firewall rules on both ends |\n| Time to stabilize | Wait 60 seconds after power-up |\n\n## Step 1: Verify Device Instance Uniqueness\n\nEvery BACnet device on an internetwork must have a unique device instance number (0 through 4194302). Duplicate device instances cause unpredictable behavior: one device may shadow another, discovery may return the wrong device, or neither device may respond consistently.\n\n### How to Check\n\n1. Send a Who-Is for the specific device instance number.\n2. If two or more I-Am responses arrive from different MAC addresses, you have a conflict.\n3. Change one device''s instance number to a unique value.\n\n### Prevention\n\nMaintain a device instance registry for the entire BACnet internetwork. Assign ranges by building, floor, or system to prevent overlaps:\n\n```\nExample Device Instance Allocation:\n  Building A, Floor 1: 100001 - 100999\n  Building A, Floor 2: 101001 - 101999\n  Building B:          200001 - 200999\n  Supervisors:         1 - 99\n```\n\n## Step 2: Check Network Numbers\n\nBACnet network numbers identify distinct network segments. Each physical or logical network must have a unique network number across the entire BACnet internetwork.\n\n| Problem | Symptom | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Duplicate network numbers | Devices on different segments appear unreachable or swap identities | Reassign one network to a unique number |\n| Network number set to 0 | Device is on a local-only network, invisible to routers | Assign a valid network number (1-65534) |\n| Missing router configuration | Who-Is does not cross segment boundaries | Configure BACnet router with correct network numbers on each port |\n\n## Step 3: Confirm Broadcast Forwarding (BBMD)\n\nOn routed IP networks, BACnet/IP broadcasts do not cross subnet boundaries without a BBMD (BACnet Broadcast Management Device). If a device is on a different subnet from the workstation performing discovery, BBMD configuration is required.\n\n### Verification Checklist\n\n```\n[ ] BBMD enabled on at least one device per BACnet/IP subnet\n[ ] All BBMDs share a complete Broadcast Distribution Table (BDT)\n[ ] BDT entries use correct IP addresses and broadcast masks\n[ ] Foreign Device Registration (FDR) configured for devices\n    not on a BACnet/IP subnet (service laptops, remote workstations)\n[ ] UDP port 47808 open bidirectionally between all BBMD hosts\n```\n\n## Step 4: Verify Router Configuration\n\nBACnet routers connect different network types (e.g., BACnet/IP to BACnet MS/TP). If a router is misconfigured, devices behind it are invisible to the rest of the internetwork.\n\n| Check | Expected |\n|---|---|\n| Router has correct network number on each port | Unique per segment |\n| Router is passing Who-Is/I-Am traffic | Not filtered or rate-limited |\n| MS/TP baud rate matches all devices on the trunk | Consistent across segment |\n| Router appears in Who-Is-Router-To-Network responses | Confirms routing table is populated |\n\n### Using Who-Is-Router-To-Network\n\nThis network-layer service asks routers to report which networks they can reach. Use it to verify:\n\n- The router knows about the target device''s network\n- No routing loops exist (same network reachable via multiple paths without proper configuration)\n- The routing table is complete\n\n## Step 5: Protocol Analyzer Diagnosis\n\nWhen the above checks do not resolve the issue, capture network traffic:\n\n1. Run a BACnet protocol analyzer (e.g., Wireshark with BACnet dissector) on the same subnet as the workstation.\n2. Send a Who-Is for the target device instance.\n3. Look for:\n   - Who-Is packet leaving the workstation (confirms the request was sent)\n   - Who-Is arriving at the target subnet (confirms BBMD/router forwarded it)\n   - I-Am response from the target device (confirms device received and responded)\n   - I-Am arriving back at the workstation (confirms return path works)\n\n### Common Findings\n\n| Capture Result | Interpretation | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| Who-Is sent, no I-Am received | Device did not receive request or did not respond | Check BBMD, router, device BACnet config |\n| I-Am from wrong device instance | Duplicate device instance | Resolve conflict |\n| I-Am received but supervisor does not list device | Supervisor filtering or capacity issue | Check supervisor discovery settings |\n| Who-Is not sent | Workstation BACnet driver issue | Restart BACnet service, check port binding |\n\n## Step 6: Device-Specific Issues\n\n| Issue | Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Device only responds to Who-Is after reboot | BACnet stack initialization delay | Allow 60-120 seconds after power-up |\n| Device responds to some workstations but not others | Network path issue specific to one workstation | Check BBMD, FDR, and firewall on the failing path |\n| Device discovered but shows \"communication failure\" | APDU timeout or segmentation mismatch | Increase APDU timeout, check max APDU size |\n| Device appears intermittently | Token passing issue (MS/TP) or network congestion (IP) | Check MS/TP wiring or IP bandwidth |\n\n## References\n\n- [ASHRAE Standard 135 (BACnet)](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/bacnet)\n- [BACnet International - About the BACnet Standard](https://bacnet.org/about-bacnet-standard/)\n- [The Language of BACnet - Objects, Properties and Services (PDF)](https://bacnet.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/4/2022/06/The-Language-of-BACnet-1.pdf)\n- [BACnet Developer Aids](https://bacnet.org/developer-aids/)\n\nMost BACnet discovery failures are network problems, not device problems. Work from the network layer up: verify the path exists before questioning the endpoint.',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO wiki_article_facets (article_id, facet_id)
SELECT
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_articles WHERE slug = 'bacnet-device-discovery-who-is-troubleshooting'),
  f.id
FROM wiki_facets f
WHERE f.slug IN ('bacnet', 'networking', 'troubleshooting')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 2: Economizer Control Verification and Fault Diagnosis
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'troubleshooting'),
  'Economizer Control Verification and Fault Diagnosis',
  'economizer-control-verification-fault-diagnosis',
  'Field-focused troubleshooting guide for air-side economizer faults, covering damper operation, sensor validation, enable/disable logic, and minimum outdoor air verification per ASHRAE 90.1 and Guideline 36.',
  E'# Economizer Control Verification and Fault Diagnosis\n\nEconomizer problems waste more energy than most technicians realize. A stuck-open outdoor air damper in winter can double heating costs. A stuck-closed damper in mild weather forces mechanical cooling when free cooling is available. This guide provides a systematic approach to verifying economizer operation.\n\n## What the Economizer Does\n\nAn air-side economizer modulates outdoor air and return air dampers to use outdoor air for free cooling when conditions permit. ASHRAE Standard 90.1 requires economizers on most cooling systems above a specified capacity threshold, depending on climate zone.\n\n## Economizer Operating Modes\n\n| Mode | Outdoor Air Damper | Return Air Damper | Mechanical Cooling |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Minimum OA (heating/deadband) | At minimum position | Nearly fully open | Off |\n| Free cooling (full economizer) | Modulating toward 100% | Modulating toward closed | Off |\n| Partial economizer + mech. cooling | Modulating | Modulating | Staging as needed |\n| Mechanical cooling only (economizer disabled) | At minimum position | Nearly fully open | Full staging |\n\n## Step 1: Verify Outdoor Air Sensor Accuracy\n\nThe economizer enable/disable decision depends on accurate outdoor air measurement. A faulty sensor can lock the economizer in the wrong mode.\n\n### Temperature Sensor Check\n\n1. Compare the BAS outdoor air temperature (OAT) reading to a calibrated reference thermometer at the sensor location.\n2. Acceptable tolerance: within 2 degF (1 degC) of the reference.\n3. Check for sensor placement issues:\n   - Direct sunlight on the sensor (reads high)\n   - Near exhaust outlets (reads high or low depending on source)\n   - Inside an enclosure with poor airflow (reads stale values)\n\n### Enthalpy Sensor Check (if used)\n\nSome economizer strategies use enthalpy (temperature + humidity) comparison:\n\n1. Compare BAS humidity reading to a calibrated reference.\n2. Acceptable tolerance: within 5% RH of reference.\n3. Humidity sensors drift over time and should be recalibrated or replaced per manufacturer recommendations.\n\n## Step 2: Verify Economizer Enable/Disable Logic\n\nThe economizer should enable when outdoor conditions are favorable for free cooling and disable when they are not.\n\n### Common Enable Strategies\n\n| Strategy | Enable Condition | Disable Condition | Standard Reference |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fixed dry-bulb | OAT < high limit (e.g., 75 degF) | OAT >= high limit | ASHRAE 90.1 |\n| Differential dry-bulb | OAT < Return Air Temp | OAT >= Return Air Temp | ASHRAE 90.1 |\n| Fixed enthalpy | OA enthalpy < high limit | OA enthalpy >= high limit | ASHRAE 90.1 |\n| Differential enthalpy | OA enthalpy < RA enthalpy | OA enthalpy >= RA enthalpy | ASHRAE 90.1 |\n\n### Verification Procedure\n\n1. Check the BAS economizer enable status.\n2. Compare to current outdoor conditions.\n3. If the economizer is enabled when it should be disabled (or vice versa), check:\n   - Sensor accuracy (Step 1)\n   - High limit setpoint in the BAS\n   - Enable strategy selection matches the specification\n   - No overrides are forcing the economizer state\n\n## Step 3: Verify Damper Operation\n\nDamper mechanical failures are common and often go undetected for months.\n\n### Physical Inspection\n\n1. Command the outdoor air damper to 0% from the BAS.\n2. Physically verify the damper blades are fully closed.\n3. Command to 100% and verify fully open.\n4. Command to 50% and verify approximately half open.\n5. Check that the damper moves smoothly through the full stroke.\n\n### Common Damper Failures\n\n| Symptom | Likely Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Damper does not move | Actuator linkage disconnected | Reconnect linkage |\n| Damper moves but does not reach full stroke | Linkage binding, blade obstruction | Inspect and clear |\n| Damper position does not match command | Actuator feedback out of calibration | Recalibrate or replace |\n| Damper oscillates or hunts | PID tuning issue or loose linkage | Tighten linkage, retune loop |\n| Damper blades visibly damaged or bent | Physical damage, corrosion | Replace damper section |\n\n### Minimum Position Verification\n\nASHRAE Standard 62.1 requires minimum outdoor airflow for ventilation. Verify:\n\n1. With economizer disabled, the outdoor air damper holds at its minimum position.\n2. Measure actual outdoor airflow and compare to the design minimum.\n3. If airflow is significantly below design, the minimum position setting may need adjustment.\n\n## Step 4: Verify Mixed Air Temperature Control\n\nDuring economizer operation, the BAS modulates dampers to maintain a mixed air temperature (MAT) setpoint.\n\n### MAT Sensor Check\n\n1. The MAT sensor should be located downstream of the mixing plenum where outdoor and return air blend.\n2. Verify the sensor reads between the outdoor air temperature and return air temperature.\n3. If MAT reads outside this range, suspect sensor error or air stratification in the mixing plenum.\n\n### Control Loop Check\n\n1. Trend MAT, OAT, damper position, and cooling stage over a period that includes economizer transitions.\n2. During free cooling, MAT should track the supply air temperature setpoint by modulating dampers.\n3. If MAT oscillates, the economizer control loop may need PID tuning.\n\n## Step 5: Verify Economizer Integration with Mechanical Cooling\n\nASHRAE Guideline 36 sequences specify that the economizer should be the first stage of cooling, with mechanical cooling supplementing only when the economizer cannot meet the load alone.\n\n### Expected Staging\n\n```\nCooling Demand Increases:\n  1. Outdoor air damper modulates open (free cooling)\n  2. If MAT still above setpoint at 100% OA → Stage 1 mechanical cooling\n  3. Additional mechanical stages as needed\n  4. Outdoor air damper may modulate back if OAT rises above RAT\n\nCooling Demand Decreases:\n  1. Mechanical stages shed first\n  2. Outdoor air damper modulates toward minimum\n```\n\n### Common Integration Faults\n\n| Fault | Effect | Diagnosis |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mechanical cooling starts before economizer is fully open | Wasted energy | Check staging sequence and interlock logic |\n| Economizer stays open when mechanical cooling is running and OAT > RAT | Increased cooling load | Check economizer disable logic |\n| Economizer disabled entirely | No free cooling benefit | Check enable logic, sensor, and overrides |\n\n## Step 6: Seasonal and Weather-Dependent Checks\n\nEconomizer faults are seasonal. Test during transitional weather (spring and fall) when the economizer should be active.\n\n| Season | Expected Behavior | What to Check |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mild weather (55-65 degF OAT) | Full economizer, minimal mechanical cooling | Damper modulation, MAT tracking |\n| Hot weather (>75 degF OAT) | Economizer disabled, minimum OA, mechanical cooling | High limit disable, min position |\n| Cold weather (<40 degF OAT) | Minimum OA, heating mode | Freeze protection, min position |\n| Humid weather | Enthalpy-based disable (if applicable) | Humidity sensor accuracy |\n\n## References\n\n- [ASHRAE Standard 90.1 - Energy Standard for Buildings](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/standard-90-1)\n- [ASHRAE Guideline 36 - High Performance Sequences of Operation for HVAC Systems](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/guideline-36-high-performance-sequences-of-operation-for-hvac-systems)\n- [ASHRAE Standard 62.1 - Ventilation for Acceptable Indoor Air Quality](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/standards-62-1-62-2)\n- [ASHRAE Guideline 13 - Specifying Building Automation Systems](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/ashrae-guideline-13-specifying-building-automation-systems)\n\nA properly functioning economizer is one of the highest-value energy conservation measures in commercial HVAC. Verifying it works correctly during commissioning and maintaining it through regular service prevents silent energy waste.',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO wiki_article_facets (article_id, facet_id)
SELECT
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_articles WHERE slug = 'economizer-control-verification-fault-diagnosis'),
