-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 4: BAS Point Naming and Tagging Convention Guide
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'best-practices'),
  'BAS Point Naming and Tagging Convention Guide',
  'bas-point-naming-tagging-convention-guide',
  'Practical guide to establishing consistent BAS point naming conventions and semantic tagging strategies, grounded in ASHRAE Guideline 36 naming patterns and Project Haystack tagging methodology.',
  E'# BAS Point Naming and Tagging Convention Guide\n\nInconsistent point naming is one of the most persistent problems in building automation. When a technician cannot tell what a point represents from its name, every troubleshooting task takes longer. When an analytics platform cannot parse point names into equipment and measurement types, data integration fails. A naming convention established before the first controller is programmed prevents years of cleanup.\n\n## Why Naming Conventions Matter\n\n| Without Convention | With Convention |\n|---|---|\n| AHU1_SAT, AHU-1.SA.T, ahu1SupTemp | AHU-1.SA-T |\n| Technician must open each point to understand it | Name describes the point at a glance |\n| Analytics requires custom parsing per site | Standard parser works across sites |\n| Point lists are unsortable and unsearchable | Consistent hierarchy enables filtering |\n| New technicians require extensive onboarding | Names are self-documenting |\n\n## Naming Convention Structure\n\nA well-designed point name encodes a hierarchy from general to specific:\n\n```\n[Building]-[System]-[Equipment].[Subsystem]-[Measurement]-[Type]\n\nExamples:\n  BLDG1-AHU-1.SA-T           (Building 1, AHU-1, Supply Air Temperature)\n  BLDG1-AHU-1.RA-T           (Return Air Temperature)\n  BLDG1-AHU-1.SF-SPD         (Supply Fan Speed)\n  BLDG1-AHU-1.SF-STS         (Supply Fan Status)\n  BLDG1-AHU-1.SF-CMD         (Supply Fan Command)\n  BLDG1-AHU-1.CLG-VLV        (Cooling Valve Position)\n  BLDG1-AHU-1.OA-DMPR        (Outdoor Air Damper Position)\n  BLDG1-VAV-1-01.ZN-T        (VAV Box 1-01, Zone Temperature)\n  BLDG1-VAV-1-01.DA-CFM      (Discharge Air CFM)\n  BLDG1-VAV-1-01.DA-DMPR     (Discharge Air Damper)\n  BLDG1-VAV-1-01.HTG-VLV     (Heating Valve)\n```\n\n## ASHRAE Guideline 36 Point Name Patterns\n\nASHRAE Guideline 36 defines control sequences that reference specific point names. While it does not mandate a naming standard, the point references in the sequences provide a de facto vocabulary:\n\n### Common Abbreviations from Guideline 36\n\n| Abbreviation | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| SA | Supply Air |\n| RA | Return Air |\n| OA | Outdoor Air |\n| MA | Mixed Air |\n| DA | Discharge Air |\n| EA | Exhaust Air |\n| ZN | Zone |\n| T | Temperature |\n| RH | Relative Humidity |\n| SP | Setpoint |\n| CFM | Airflow (cubic feet per minute) |\n| VLV | Valve |\n| DMPR | Damper |\n| CMD | Command |\n| STS | Status |\n| SPD | Speed |\n| ALM | Alarm |\n| HTG | Heating |\n| CLG | Cooling |\n| SF | Supply Fan |\n| RF | Return Fan |\n| EF | Exhaust Fan |\n| CHW | Chilled Water |\n| HHW | Heating Hot Water |\n| CW | Condenser Water |\n\n## Delimiter and Case Rules\n\nPick one convention and enforce it across the entire project:\n\n| Rule | Recommended | Avoid |\n|---|---|\n| Hierarchy delimiter | Hyphen (-) or period (.) | Spaces, underscores mixed with hyphens |\n| Case | UPPER or Title case consistently | Mixed case without rules |\n| Spaces | Never | Creates parsing problems |\n| Special characters | Avoid | Causes issues in some BAS platforms |\n| Maximum length | 30-50 characters | Too short loses meaning, too long truncates |\n\n## Semantic Tagging with Project Haystack\n\nProject Haystack takes a different approach from hierarchical naming. Instead of encoding meaning into the point name string, Haystack attaches structured tags (key-value pairs and markers) to each entity.\n\n### Haystack Tagging Model\n\nHaystack organizes building data into three primary entity types:\n\n| Entity | Marker Tag | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Site | `site` | A building or facility |\n| Equipment | `equip` | A piece of mechanical equipment (AHU, VAV, boiler) |\n| Point | `point` | A sensor, command, or setpoint on equipment |\n\nEach entity carries tags that describe it. Points include:\n\n- **siteRef**: Reference to the parent site\n- **equipRef**: Reference to the parent equipment\n- **Point function marker**: Exactly one of `sensor`, `cmd`, or `sp`\n- **Substance and measurement tags**: e.g., `air`, `temp`, `discharge`, `flow`\n\n### Example: Haystack Tags for an AHU Supply Air Temperature Sensor\n\n```\nTags:\n  point\n  sensor\n  air\n  temp\n  discharge\n  equipRef: @ahu-1\n  siteRef: @building-1\n  unit: degF\n  kind: Number\n```\n\n### Names vs. Tags\n\n| Approach | Strength | Weakness |\n|---|---|---|\n| Hierarchical naming | Human-readable at a glance, works in any BAS | Meaning is implicit, parsing is fragile |\n| Semantic tagging (Haystack) | Machine-readable, consistent across systems | Requires tagging infrastructure |\n| Both | Best of both worlds | More upfront effort |\n\nThe practical recommendation is to use both: a consistent naming convention for day-to-day BAS operation, and semantic tags for analytics and cross-system integration.\n\n## Implementation Checklist\n\n```\nBefore Programming Begins:\n  [ ] Naming convention documented and approved\n  [ ] Abbreviation table published\n  [ ] Delimiter and case rules defined\n  [ ] Equipment naming scheme aligned with mechanical drawings\n  [ ] Point naming scheme reviewed against sequence of operations\n  [ ] Maximum name length validated against BAS platform limits\n  [ ] Sample point names reviewed with operations staff\n  [ ] Naming convention included in project specification\n\nDuring Programming:\n  [ ] Point names match the approved convention\n  [ ] Equipment names match mechanical schedules\n  [ ] No duplicate names within a controller or site\n  [ ] Engineering units consistent (degF vs degC, CFM vs L/s)\n\nDuring Commissioning:\n  [ ] Point list export matches as-built documentation\n  [ ] Naming convention audit before turnover\n  [ ] Operations staff can identify points by name alone\n```\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | Problem | Prevention |\n|---|---|---|\n| No convention at project start | Every programmer uses their own style | Include convention in project spec |\n| Convention exists but is not enforced | Drift over time, especially with subcontractors | Audit during commissioning |\n| Names are too short | AH1T1 is meaningless to the next technician | Use readable abbreviations |\n| Names are too long | Truncation in BAS displays and exports | Set maximum length |\n| Encoding location in point name instead of hierarchy | Floor-3-Room-301-VAV-... becomes unusable | Use equipment hierarchy, not spatial |\n| Inconsistent abbreviations | TEMP, Temp, T, Tmp all mean temperature | Publish and enforce abbreviation table |\n\n## References\n\n- [ASHRAE Guideline 36 - High Performance Sequences of Operation for HVAC Systems](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/guideline-36-high-performance-sequences-of-operation-for-hvac-systems)\n- [Project Haystack - Introduction](https://project-haystack.org/doc/docHaystack/Intro)\n- [Project Haystack - Points](https://project-haystack.org/doc/docHaystack/Points)\n- [Project Haystack - Equipment](https://project-haystack.org/doc/docHaystack/Equips)\n- [ASHRAE Guideline 13 - Specifying Building Automation Systems](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/ashrae-guideline-13-specifying-building-automation-systems)\n\nA naming convention is the cheapest investment in a BAS project and the most expensive to retrofit. Define it once, enforce it always.',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO wiki_article_facets (article_id, facet_id)
SELECT
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_articles WHERE slug = 'bas-point-naming-tagging-convention-guide'),
  f.id
FROM wiki_facets f
WHERE f.slug IN ('tagging', 'commissioning', 'integration')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 5: Niagara Workbench and Platform Connection Troubleshooting
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'troubleshooting'),
  'Niagara Workbench and Platform Connection Troubleshooting',
  'niagara-workbench-platform-connection-troubleshooting',
  'Decision-tree troubleshooting for Niagara Workbench connection failures, including fox/foxs port issues, credential problems, certificate trust, network connectivity, and platform daemon state.',
  E'# Niagara Workbench and Platform Connection Troubleshooting\n\nWorkbench is the primary client for configuring and managing Niagara stations. When it cannot connect to a station or platform, every other task stops. This guide provides a systematic approach to resolving Workbench connection failures.\n\n## Connection Architecture\n\nWorkbench connects to Niagara stations using the Fox protocol:\n\n| Protocol | Port | Transport | Usage |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fox (unencrypted) | 1911 (TCP) | Plain TCP | Legacy, not recommended for production |\n| Foxs (TLS-encrypted) | 4911 (TCP) | TLS over TCP | Recommended for all deployments |\n| HTTP/HTTPS | 80/443 (TCP) | HTTP(S) | Web browser access to station |\n\nWorkbench → Station connections require:\n1. Network connectivity (TCP) to the station IP and port\n2. Valid credentials (username and password)\n3. Certificate trust (for foxs/HTTPS connections)\n4. Platform daemon running on the station\n5. Station running (for station-level connections)\n\n## Step 1: Verify Network Connectivity\n\nBefore troubleshooting Niagara-specific issues, confirm basic network access.\n\n### Checks\n\n| Test | Command / Method | Expected Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ping station IP | `ping <station-ip>` | Replies received |\n| TCP port open (foxs) | `telnet <station-ip> 4911` or port scan | Connection accepted |\n| TCP port open (fox) | `telnet <station-ip> 1911` | Connection accepted |\n| TCP port open (HTTPS) | `telnet <station-ip> 443` | Connection accepted |\n| DNS resolution (if using hostname) | `nslookup <hostname>` | Resolves to correct IP |\n\n### Network Failure Patterns\n\n| Symptom | Likely Cause |\n|---|---|\n| Ping fails | Wrong IP, device powered off, VLAN issue, or ICMP blocked |\n| Ping succeeds but port refused | Correct IP but Fox/platform service not running |\n| Ping succeeds but port times out | Firewall blocking the port |\n| Intermittent connectivity | Network congestion, unstable link, or DHCP lease changing |\n\n## Step 2: Verify Platform Daemon Status\n\nThe platform daemon must be running before Workbench can connect. If the station is accessible via its web interface but Workbench cannot connect via Fox, the platform daemon may be stopped.\n\n### Recovery Options\n\n| Situation | Recovery |\n|---|---|\n| Physical access to JACE/controller | Use hardware buttons or console port to restart platform |\n| Web interface accessible | Navigate to platform administration to check daemon status |\n| SSH/console access available | Use command-line tools to restart the platform daemon |\n| No access at all | Power cycle the controller (last resort) |\n\n## Step 3: Verify Credentials\n\nWorkbench requires valid platform or station credentials. Authentication failures are common after password changes, staff turnover, or security hardening.\n\n### Common Credential Issues\n\n| Problem | Symptom | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| Wrong password | \"Authentication failed\" error | Verify password, check caps lock |\n| Account locked | \"Account locked\" or repeated auth failures | Unlock via another admin account or platform recovery |\n| Account expired | \"Account expired\" error | Reset expiration via admin account |\n| No admin account known | Cannot log in at all | Use platform recovery procedure (requires physical access and serial number for JACE-8000, Niagara 4.4+) |\n| Default credentials changed | Old credentials no longer work | Retrieve current credentials from documentation or password manager |\n\n## Step 4: Verify Certificate Trust (Foxs/HTTPS)\n\nWhen connecting via foxs or HTTPS, Workbench must trust the station''s TLS certificate. Certificate issues are the most common cause of post-hardening connection failures.\n\n### Certificate Error Patterns\n\n| Error | Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"Certificate not trusted\" | Station certificate not in Workbench trust store | Approve or import certificate in Workbench |\n| \"Certificate has expired\" | Station certificate past expiry date | Generate new certificate on station, re-approve in Workbench |\n| \"Hostname mismatch\" | Connecting by IP but certificate issued to hostname (or vice versa) | Use the name/IP that matches the certificate, or regenerate certificate |\n| \"Cannot verify certificate chain\" | Missing intermediate CA certificate | Import full certificate chain into Workbench trust store |\n\n### Certificate Approval in Workbench\n\nWhen connecting to a station with a self-signed certificate for the first time, Workbench prompts to approve the certificate. If the certificate changes (regenerated, renewed, or replaced), Workbench shows a warning and requires re-approval.\n\nThe same certificate trust mechanism applies to:\n- Station-to-station connections (driver links)\n- Workbench help system (\"Cannot Load Help\" errors)\n- Web browser access (browser-level certificate warnings)\n\n## Step 5: Verify Protocol and Port Selection\n\nMismatched protocol and port combinations are a common source of connection failures, especially after security hardening.\n\n| Connection Target | Correct Protocol | Correct Port |\n|---|---|---|\n| Platform (pre-hardening) | fox | 1911 |\n| Platform (post-hardening) | foxs | 4911 |\n| Station (pre-hardening) | fox | 1911 |\n| Station (post-hardening) | foxs | 4911 |\n| Web UI (pre-hardening) | http | 80 |\n| Web UI (post-hardening) | https | 443 |\n\nAfter enabling HTTPS-only mode on a station, all fox (unencrypted) connections are rejected. Update Workbench connection entries to use foxs.\n\n## Step 6: Firewall and IT Infrastructure Issues\n\n| Issue | Diagnostic | Resolution |\n|---|---|---|\n| Corporate firewall blocking Fox ports | Port scan shows closed ports | Request firewall rule for TCP 4911 (foxs) |\n| VPN required for remote access | Connection fails from outside the building network | Connect to VPN first |\n| NAT translation breaking Fox | Connection initiates but drops | Check NAT rules for Fox port forwarding |\n| Proxy server intercepting HTTPS | Certificate errors from proxy, not station | Bypass proxy for BAS subnet or add proxy CA to trust store |\n\n## Step 7: Workbench-Side Issues\n\n| Problem | Symptom | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Workbench version too old for station | Incompatible protocol version | Update Workbench to match station version |\n| Multiple Workbench instances conflicting | Port binding errors | Close extra instances |\n| Workbench Java runtime issue | Crash or hang during connection | Clear Workbench cache, reinstall if needed |\n| Workbench trust store corrupted | All foxs connections fail | Reset Workbench user trust store |\n\n## References\n\n- [Tridium Documents Library](https://www.tridium.com/us/en/services-support/library)\n- [Tridium Niagara Framework](https://www.tridium.com/us/en/Products/niagara)\n- [NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3 - Guide to Operational Technology Security](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/82/r3/final)\n\nWorkbench connection problems are almost always network, credential, or certificate issues. Work through the layers systematically and you will find the cause before reaching step 7.',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO wiki_article_facets (article_id, facet_id)
SELECT
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_articles WHERE slug = 'niagara-workbench-platform-connection-troubleshooting'),
  f.id
FROM wiki_facets f
WHERE f.slug IN ('niagara', 'troubleshooting', 'networking')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 6: Common BACnet Object Types Quick Reference
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'reference'),
  'Common BACnet Object Types Quick Reference',
  'common-bacnet-object-types-quick-reference',
  'Quick reference table of the most common BACnet object types defined in ASHRAE Standard 135, including their properties, typical use cases, and field troubleshooting notes.',
  E'# Common BACnet Object Types Quick Reference\n\nBACnet abstracts building automation data into objects. Each physical sensor, actuator, setpoint, schedule, or trend log is represented by a standard object type with defined properties. Understanding object types is fundamental to configuring, troubleshooting, and integrating BACnet systems.\n\nThis reference covers the object types most commonly encountered in HVAC building automation, as defined in ASHRAE Standard 135.\n\n## Object Type Summary Table\n\n| Object Type | Abbreviation | Typical Use | Commandable |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Analog Input | AI | Physical sensor reading (temperature, pressure, humidity) | No |\n| Analog Output | AO | Physical actuator output (valve, damper, VFD speed) | Yes |\n| Analog Value | AV | Calculated value, setpoint, or internal variable | Yes (if configured) |\n| Binary Input | BI | Physical status contact (fan status, switch position) | No |\n| Binary Output | BO | Physical relay or digital output (fan start/stop) | Yes |\n| Binary Value | BV | Logical state, mode flag, or internal boolean | Yes (if configured) |\n| Multi-State Input | MSI | Enumerated physical status (equipment mode indicator) | No |\n| Multi-State Output | MSO | Enumerated physical command (multi-speed fan) | Yes |\n| Multi-State Value | MSV | Enumerated internal variable (operating mode selector) | Yes (if configured) |\n| Device | DEV | Represents the BACnet device itself | N/A |\n| Schedule | SCHED | Time-based schedule for equipment operation | N/A |\n| Calendar | CAL | Exception dates (holidays, special events) | N/A |\n| Trend Log | TL | Historical data recording for a point | N/A |\n| Notification Class | NC | Alarm routing and notification configuration | N/A |\n| Event Enrollment | EE | Alarm condition monitoring and state machine | N/A |\n| Loop | LOOP | PID control loop | Yes |\n\n## Analog Objects\n\n### Analog Input (AI)\n\nRepresents a physical analog sensor input.\n\n| Property | Description | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Present-Value | Current sensor reading in engineering units | 72.5 degF |\n| Units | Engineering units (from BACnet enumeration) | degrees-fahrenheit |\n| Out-Of-Service | If true, Present-Value is manually set, not from hardware | false |\n| Status-Flags | Alarm, fault, overridden, out-of-service indicators | {false, false, false, false} |\n| Reliability | Sensor reliability status | no-fault-detected |\n| Min/Max-Pres-Value | Operating range | 0 / 150 degF |\n| COV-Increment | Change-of-value threshold for COV subscriptions | 0.5 degF |\n\n### Analog Output (AO)\n\nRepresents a physical analog output to an actuator.\n\n| Property | Description | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Present-Value | Current output command in engineering units | 45.0 % |\n| Priority-Array | 16-level priority array for command arbitration | See Priority Array section |\n| Relinquish-Default | Value used when no priority level is active | 0.0 % |\n| Units | Engineering units | percent |\n\n### Analog Value (AV)\n\nRepresents a calculated value, setpoint, or internal parameter. Not tied to physical hardware.\n\n| Common Uses |\n|---|\n| Zone temperature setpoint |\n| Calculated efficiency value |\n| PID tuning parameter |\n| Sensor offset or calibration factor |\n| Schedule-driven setpoint |\n\n## Binary Objects\n\n### Binary Input (BI)\n\nRepresents a physical digital input (contact closure).\n\n| Property | Description | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Present-Value | Current state | active (1) or inactive (0) |\n| Polarity | Defines which electrical state maps to active/inactive | normal or reverse |\n| Active-Text | Display text for active state | \"Running\" |\n| Inactive-Text | Display text for inactive state | \"Stopped\" |\n\n### Binary Output (BO)\n\nRepresents a physical relay or digital output.\n\n| Property | Description | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Present-Value | Current output command | active (1) = relay energized |\n| Priority-Array | 16-level priority array | See Priority Array section |\n| Polarity | Electrical polarity mapping | normal |\n| Active-Text / Inactive-Text | Display text | \"On\" / \"Off\" |\n\n### Binary Value (BV)\n\nRepresents a logical boolean (not tied to hardware).\n\n| Common Uses |\n|---|\n| Occupied/Unoccupied mode flag |\n| Alarm enable/disable |\n| Override status |\n| Interlock condition |\n\n## Multi-State Objects\n\n### Multi-State Input (MSI)\n\nRepresents an enumerated physical input (more than two states).\n\n| Property | Description | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Present-Value | Current state (unsigned integer, 1-based) | 3 |\n| Number-Of-States | Total number of valid states | 4 |\n| State-Text | Array of text descriptions | {\"Off\", \"Low\", \"Medium\", \"High\"} |\n\n### Multi-State Output (MSO)\n\nRepresents an enumerated physical output.\n\n| Common Uses |\n|---|\n| Multi-speed fan control (Off, Low, Medium, High) |\n| Chiller staging command |\n| Damper position preset selection |\n\n### Multi-State Value (MSV)\n\nRepresents an enumerated internal variable.\n\n| Common Uses |\n|---|\n| Operating mode selection (Heating, Cooling, Auto, Off) |\n| Equipment state machine |\n| Alarm severity level |\n\n## Priority Array\n\nCommandable objects (AO, BO, MSO, AV, BV, MSV when configured) use a 16-level priority array to resolve conflicting commands. Lower numbers are higher priority.\n\n| Priority | Intended Use |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Manual-Life Safety |\n| 2 | Automatic-Life Safety |\n| 3 | Available |\n| 4 | Available |\n| 5 | Critical Equipment Control |\n| 6 | Minimum On/Off |\n| 7 | Available |\n| 8 | Manual Operator |\n| 9 | Available |\n| 10 | Available |\n| 11 | Available |\n| 12 | Available |\n| 13 | Available |\n| 14 | Available |\n| 15 | Available |\n| 16 | Available |\n\nWhen a priority level is written, it overrides all lower priorities. When a priority level is relinquished (set to NULL), the next lower active priority takes effect. If no priorities are active, the Relinquish-Default value is used.\n\n### Common Priority Array Troubleshooting\n\n| Symptom | Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Output does not respond to BAS command | Higher-priority command is active | Read priority array to find the active priority level |\n| Output stuck after override cleared | Override was written at a priority that was not relinquished | Write NULL to the override priority level |\n| Multiple systems fighting over an output | Both writing at the same priority | Assign different priority levels to each system |\n\n## Schedule and Calendar Objects\n\n### Schedule (SCHED)\n\nControls time-based operation by writing values to referenced objects at scheduled times.\n\n| Property | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Weekly-Schedule | Seven-day schedule with time-value pairs |\n| Exception-Schedule | Overrides weekly schedule for specific dates or date ranges |\n| Schedule-Default | Value applied when no schedule entry is active |\n| List-Of-Object-Property-References | Objects and properties controlled by this schedule |\n\n### Calendar (CAL)\n\nDefines a list of dates or date ranges (holidays, special events) that schedules can reference.\n\n| Property | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Date-List | Array of dates, date ranges, and week-and-day entries |\n| Present-Value | True if today matches any entry in the Date-List |\n\n## Trend Log (TL)\n\nRecords historical values of a monitored object property.\n\n| Property | Description |\n|---|---|\n| Log-Buffer | Circular buffer of timestamped records |\n| Log-Device-Object-Property | The object and property being trended |\n| Log-Interval | Sampling interval (0 = triggered by COV) |\n| Buffer-Size | Maximum number of records before overwrite |\n| Record-Count | Current number of records in the buffer |\n| Total-Record-Count | Total records since the log was enabled |\n\n## References\n\n- [ASHRAE Standard 135 (BACnet)](https://www.ashrae.org/technical-resources/bookstore/bacnet)\n- [ASHRAE Standard 135 Resource Files](https://data.ashrae.org/bacnet/)\n- [BACnet International - About the BACnet Standard](https://bacnet.org/about-bacnet-standard/)\n- [The Language of BACnet - Objects, Properties and Services (PDF)](https://bacnet.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/4/2022/06/The-Language-of-BACnet-1.pdf)\n- [BACnet Developer Aids](https://bacnet.org/developer-aids/)\n\nKnowing what each BACnet object type represents and how its properties work is the difference between reading a BACnet network fluently and staring at numbers without context.',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO wiki_article_facets (article_id, facet_id)
SELECT
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_articles WHERE slug = 'common-bacnet-object-types-quick-reference'),
  f.id
FROM wiki_facets f
WHERE f.slug IN ('bacnet', 'integration')
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Update facet article_count from actual data
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE wiki_facets f
SET article_count = COALESCE(counts.cnt, 0)
FROM (
  SELECT facet_id, COUNT(*) as cnt
  FROM wiki_article_facets
  GROUP BY facet_id
) counts
WHERE f.id = counts.facet_id;

UPDATE wiki_facets SET article_count = 0
WHERE id NOT IN (SELECT DISTINCT facet_id FROM wiki_article_facets);
